Given:
$$f\left(x\right)=\frac{1}{3}x^{3}-{f'}\left(2\right)x^{2}+x-3$$

Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we get:
\begin{align*}
{f'}\left(x\right) &= \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{1}{3}x^{3}\right) - \frac{d}{dx}\left({f'}\left(2\right)x^{2}\right) + \frac{d}{dx}\left(x\right) - \frac{d}{dx}\left(3\right) \\
&= x^{2} - 2{f'}\left(2\right)x + 1
\end{align*}

Now, substituting $x = 2$ into ${f'}\left(x\right)$:
\begin{align*}
{f'}\left(2\right) &= 2^{2} - 2{f'}\left(2\right)\cdot2 + 1 \\
&= 4 - 4{f'}\left(2\right) + 1 \\
&= 5 - 4{f'}\left(2\right)
\end{align*}

Solving the equation $5 - 4{f'}\left(2\right) = {f'}\left(2\right)$ for ${f'}\left(2\right)$:
\begin{align*}
5 &= 5{f'}\left(2\right) \\
{f'}\left(2\right) &= 1
\end{align*}

Therefore, the correct answer is $\boxed{B}$.

